A significant aspect of Clifford Analysis is the Clifford Dirac Operator, denoted as $\Delta_{c}$, which has been extensively studied and its eigenfunctions $\phi_{\lambda}$ demonstrated for a particular distribution $f$ that $\Delta_{c}$ is closely related to the differential operator $\Delta_{c} \phi_{\lambda} = \lambda \phi_{\lambda}$. Furthermore, investigations have been made into the properties of the eigenfunctions $\phi_{\lambda}$, including the derivation of a generating function, applications to the solution of initial value problems, and the identification of the spectrum $\lambda$ as being real with multiplicity two, denoted as $sp(\lambda) = \{\lambda_{1}, \lambda_{2}\}$. In this paper, a specific arrangement of the eigenfunctions $\phi_{\lambda}$, characterized by the condition $\lambda_{1} 
eq 0$, is examined to prove that the sum of the absolute values of three distinct points on a hyperbola-shaped curve is greater than the largest absolute value of any point on the curve, effectively proving the three balls conjecture for these particular eigenfunctions. Although this result does not provide evidence for the alleged property of $\lambda_{1} 
eq 0$ being true for all $\lambda$, it does offer a crucial counterexample to the proposed property. Additionally, this finding can be applied to demonstrate that the three balls conjecture is an unstable property, subject to variation as the index $\lambda$ changes. Moreover, this paper also presents an alternative, more concise proof for the specific arrangement of the eigenfunctions $\phi_{\lambda}$, denoted as the $S$-arrangement, with the condition $\lambda_{1} 
eq 0$, with the added benefit of providing an insightful comparison between the original and the new proof approaches. It is worth noting that the term "stability" in this context refers to the sensitivity of the three balls conjecture to the changes in the index $\lambda$. This notion of stability can be visually represented through a graph plotting the index $\lambda$ against the sum of the absolute values of the three points, revealing a complex, hyperbolic structure that may have significant implications for further generalization. Finally, this paper also examines the concept of hyperbolic stability in relation to the three balls conjecture, with an emphasis on its potential applications to the related problems of hyperbolic geometry. It is anticipated that this investigation will contribute significantly to the advancement of
